WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:04.590
- [Presenter] The US is
watching Moscow closely in 2020.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.290
Two weeks before the election,

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:09.520
the FBI indicted six Russians
on charges of engaging

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.180
in cyber attacks on the
2018 Winter Olympics

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.670
and the 2017 French election.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:18.137
- No country has weaponized
its cyber capabilities

00:00:18.137 --> 00:00:22.720
as maliciously and
irresponsibly as Russia.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.150
- [Presenter] Meanwhile,
Microsoft reported

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.200
that a Russian hacker
group attacked hundreds

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.240
of political organizations and campaigns.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.610
And a Facebook investigation
discovered a network

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:33.710
of Russian trolls that paid Americans

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:36.400
to write for a fake news site.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.620
US authorities have been
cracking down to prevent

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:42.400
what happened during the 2016
US presidential election.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.460
- Russia is behind the hacks
that brought Democratic party

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.530
and Clinton campaign
emails into the public eye.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:51.340
The growing fewer over
Moscow's use of social media

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.460
to influence the 2016
presidential election.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:57.160
- [Presenter] The US
accused Russia of hacking

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.810
and trolling attempts aimed at hurting

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:01.690
the Democratic candidate in 2016.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:05.720
Moscow has denied any
interference in the US elections.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.810
- US officials experts
at social media companies

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:11.690
and researchers at
cybersecurity firms all say

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:15.770
that Russia remains a very
active and very serious threats

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:18.720
one that everybody is
very much on the lookout

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.600
for even after election day.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.440
Well, officials and
cybersecurity experts say

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.650
they haven't found as
much activity from Russia

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.870
and as we saw in the 2016,

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.160
they say Russian hackers and ensured

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.570
they have expanded their toolkit

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.690
and have become much more sophisticated

00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:34.163
and harder to detect.

00:01:35.521 --> 00:01:38.010
- [Presenter} In 2016, Russian
trolls created fake Accounts

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.660
and posted memes that
attacked Hillary Clinton.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.710
They also bought
advertisements on Facebook

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:45.210
and organized events including
ones that drew support

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:46.600
for Donald Trump.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.260
This year, the trolls
tried something different.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.210
They set up a network
of accounts and pages

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:55.070
that were largely linked to a
website called Peacedata.net.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:56.800
But to anyone who knows Russian,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.960
the website's name hinted
at it's trolling origin.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.520
When you say Peace Data quickly,

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.740
it sounds like a Russian obscenity.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.330
- Peace Data tried to look
like a credible news outlet

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:10.190
and trick people into then
relying on that as their news.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:11.680
- [Presenter] After a tip from the FBI,

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.880
Facebook said it
discovered that Peace Data

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.430
was run by people linked
to a Russian troll factory

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:18.650
called the Internet Research Agency.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:21.120
It had been accused by US
authorities of designing

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.580
a social media campaign
to provoke political

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.190
and social discourse.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:29.230
- What we found was a
website that was entirely run

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.410
by fake personas.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.980
- [Presenter] Ben Nimmo runs
investigations at Graphica,

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.020
a research firm that
contracts with Facebook

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:39.260
to analyze foreign social
media manipulation.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.530
He says the trolls in 2016
mainly created group accounts

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:46.170
but in 2020, Peace Data's
trolls got more personal.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.070
- They have profile
pictures which are generated

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:49.670
by artificial intelligence.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.930
They then had a number of
accounts across Facebook

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.620
and Twitter and LinkedIn.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.110
And what they were doing was
they were hiring real people,

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:00.280
authentic freelancers to
write content for them.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.670
- So this does a few things for them,

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:04.315
it does sort of separate Russia

00:03:04.315 --> 00:03:07.320
from the direct influence
operation in a way

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.150
that might make it harder
to track, harder to identify

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:13.540
and it also leads to
organic content in some ways

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:17.140
that seems more real, seems more authentic

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:19.160
because it's being written
by a native English speaker

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.810
who knows about American politics.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.690
- [Presenter] The Russian
embassy in Washington DC

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:27.240
said Facebook's allegations
are anti-Russia propaganda.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.400
Facebook, Twitter and
LinkedIn tipped down accounts

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.460
and pages linked to Peace Data.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:33.490
The website was eventually closed.

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:36.351
In total, the site actually
had a very small following.

00:03:36.351 --> 00:03:40.650
Overall, the traffic seems
to have not read the levels

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.180
that we saw in 2016

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.430
but that might only be
a small representation

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:46.270
of all the websites out there that Russia

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:49.850
or anyone else is using to try
to influence American voters.

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:51.870
And so if we can understand the tactics,

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.050
I think that helps us to be on alert

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:58.370
for similar kind of behavior
that might be on the platforms.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.790
After the exposure of
Peace Data, another website

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:04.000
which was exposed, which is called NAEBC

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.320
which posted as a far right
white supremacist site

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.530
was behaving in very much the same way.

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:12.653
Hiring unwitting
freelancers to write for it.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:15.270
- [Presenter] The hacks have also evolved.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.910
In September, Microsoft
said it detected activity

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.680
from the same Russian hackers
who were behind the attacks

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.320
on the Democratic campaign in 2016.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:24.970
The software giants at this time,

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.370
the hackers had new reconnaissance tools

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:30.270
instead of emailing malicious
links to steal passwords,

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.190
they deployed password sprays

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:33.840
which essentially guesses passwords

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.090
with multiple automated attempts.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:39.740
Microsoft said most of the cyber
attacks weren't successful.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.540
The Russian embassy in Washington DC

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:43.480
denied Microsoft allegations.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:46.090
- The conspiracy of Russian military and--

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:47.750
- [Presenter] Realizing
the threat this year,

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:50.610
multiple US security
agencies are working together

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:53.770
to share intelligence with
social media platforms.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:56.700
A Senate investigation
found that US tech giants

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.120
helped spread misinformation
during the 2016 election

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.940
and lawmakers pushed the
companies to better coordinate.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.400
- Heading into 2016
the idea of cooperating

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.114
on something like election interference

00:05:07.114 --> 00:05:08.947
was just not happening

00:05:09.857 --> 00:05:10.770
but this was just essentially

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:14.840
after the Edward Snowden
leaks of surveillance secrets

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:16.710
from the national security agency

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:19.860
which really increased
tensions between Silicon Valley

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.200
and the government.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.240
- [Presenter] Despite the team effort,

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:24.850
it's still difficult to
publicize Russian activities

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:26.560
and not create confusion.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.880
- Officials and companies
are constantly grappling

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.090
with the challenge of informing the public

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:35.740
about foreign interference
without being overly alarmist

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:37.760
about it and scaring them so much

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.050
that it actually accomplishes the goal

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.940
of a lot of these foreign
influence operations

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.010
which is just to sow chaos and discord

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.913
to play on the existing
tensions we have in America.

